This year, I helped Marvel put AI-assisted development into practice while taking ownership of critical CubIQ components and delivering solutions that improved how Market Risk calculates, validates, and accesses data.

**AI-assisted development and team adoption.** I use GitHub Copilot regularly to analyze requirements, research technical options, plan solutions, and generate code during implementation. I evaluated HVE Core and SpecKit and used HVE Core to build an MRV Builder proof of concept that demonstrated an agentic workflow from research and planning through implementation. I presented the workflow to more than 50 Marvel developers and implemented and validated a repeatable distribution process using internal GitLab and DevPod. This gives colleagues a consistent way to access the tools in their development workspaces and provides a foundation for the small-group pilot now underway.

**MRV Builder and CubIQ validation.** I designed and implemented MRV Builder, which generates calculations in the CubIQ Power BI semantic model from user-provided definitions. I extended its APIs to manage calculations and metadata and enable integration with the Marvel UI. MRV Builder covered 100% of existing on-premises MRVs, extended the range of supported calculations, and achieved over 98% accuracy in results. I also built a Spark-based reconciliation framework in Databricks to compare approximately 2,500 MRV calculations against the on-premises baseline. Daily reconciliation helped the team identify discrepancies earlier and verify fixes against the agreed acceptance criteria.

**OneRisk data delivery.** I partnered with OneRisk to resolve a performance bottleneck in its data-loading pipelines. I implemented a service that retrieves MRV results directly from CubIQ Delta tables using Databricks Metric Views. This enabled OneRisk to complete its loads within the agreed SLA and reduced demand on the shared Power BI semantic model.

**Marvel Self-Service and report traceability.** I developed an API that versions user changes to Power BI reports in Azure Data Lake Storage (ADLS). This gives Marvel Self-Service a version history that makes report changes traceable over time.

**Marvel Explorer and Hybrid replacement.** I designed an API that connects directly to AGrid to retrieve DS2 data and implemented the underlying remote procedure call component using the Internet Communications Engine (ICE). This enables Marvel Explorer to process DS2 requests and supports the effort to replace the Hybrid application.

**Engineering quality and release ownership.** I built the base CubIQ Power BI semantic model in TMDL and established a CI/CD process aligned with UBS release practices. I implemented unit tests that achieved over 80% code coverage on both MRV Builder and the CubIQ semantic model. I also led the upgrade of our .NET components from .NET 8 to .NET 10.
